A costume lets you catch yourself doing something you do every morning. You pick up a shirt, picture yourself walking into the room in it, and decide whether that’s how you want the room to meet you.

And the choice can be sincere. A man puts on his dad’s old jacket because he misses him. His son sees that same jacket and thinks, Dad’s taking me fishing. One garment is speaking two languages before either of them says a word.

That’s the braid again. You choose what to put on. Other people bring their own history to what they see. And after enough Saturdays on the water, your son might put that jacket on for the same reason you did.